      "rationale": "Reward 0, third consecutive Codex failure (3/3), three consecutive recorded zeroes. Service fully correct across all three trials (27/27). Checker missed the exact same candidate a third time (premature-publication, current_artifacts). Direct code reading of all three independently-written checker.mjs files confirms genuine exact recurrence: all three verify content-addressed correctness recursively (bytes match recomputed [tool, flags, source, deps]) and per-round current-handle matching, but none ever check that a handle's compile() call happened before the publish() call that references it -- only that it is eventually byte-correct. premature-publication exploits exactly that gap: it predicts the handle, publishes first, compiles second, and the final ledger state looks retroactively valid. Trial 4's checker is more rigorous than 2/3 on other dimensions (strict observation-sequence ordering, per-round compile-count cross-checks) but still never touches this specific ordering dimension. Three fresh Codex/xhigh sessions, no shared context, converged on the identical blind spot. The checker-grade summary file is byte-identical (same SHA-256) to Trial 3's, reflecting an identical accept/reject verdict on every one of the 15 candidates -- independent corroboration of the code-level finding.",
